## QUESTION: DATABASE REPLICATION KAISE HOTA HAI?
-------------------------------------------------------

Replication ka matlab hai:

Primary DB mein hone wale data changes ko
Replica(s) tak propagate/apply karna.

Replication mein har write ke baad poora database
dobara copy nahi kiya jaata.

Instead, Sirf Primary DB me hone wale naye changes
replicas tak propagate kiye jaate hain.


Example — MySQL style:

1. Primary DB par write hota hai.

2. Primary DB change ko replication/change log me record karta hai.
   MySQL me ise Binary Log (Binlog) kehte hain.

3. Replica(s) Primary ke change log se changes receive/read karti hain.

4. Replica(s) un changes ko apne database me apply karti hain.


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: PRIMARY DB PAR WRITE KE BAAD KYA APPLICATION KO SUCCESS ACK DENE SE PEHLE REPLICA(S) SE ACKNOWLEDGEMENT KA WAIT KARNA CHAHIYE?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Replication ke do common approaches samajhte hain:

1. SYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION
---------------------------

Is approach mein Primary DB par write successfully complete hone ke baad,
Primary DB Replica(s) se required acknowledgements milne ka wait karta hai.

Ye acknowledgements Replica(s) par woh change successfully
replicate/apply hone ke baad milte hain.

Required acknowledgements milne ke baad,
Primary DB Application ko write success ka acknowledgement bhejta hai,
aur Application Client ko success response deti hai.


## IMPORTANT:
---------------

"Required acknowledgements" important hain.

Synchronous Replication mein zaroori nahi ki Primary DB
har Replica ka ACK wait kare.

Write ko successfully acknowledge karne ke liye kitne
acknowledgements required hain (suppose 4 ACK chahiye), aur woh
acknowledgements specific Replica(s) se chahiye ya available
Replica(s) mein se kisi bhi 4 Replicas ke ACK chahiye —
ye database/system ki replication configuration aur policy
par depend karta hai.



## BENEFIT :
-------------

Synchronous Replication mein write ko acknowledge karne se pehle,
Primary DB Replica(s) se required acknowledgements milne ka wait karta hai.

Isliye Primary DB fail hone par, jin Replica(s) se required
acknowledgements receive ho chuke hain, unke paas latest
acknowledged write available hone ki stronger guarantee hoti hai.

Saath hi, agar subsequent read kisi aise Read Replica par jaaye
jisne latest write ke liye acknowledgement diya tha,
to us Replica par latest acknowledged write already available hoti hai.
Isliye us particular read request ke liye latest/fresh data milta hai.

Lekin agar read kisi aisi Replica par jaaye jis par latest change
abhi tak replicate/apply nahi hua hai, to stale data mil sakta hai.


## LIMITATION :
----------------

1. HIGHER WRITE LATENCY
------------------------
  Primary DB par write successfully complete hone ke baad, usko required Replica(s)
  par bhi us change ke persist/apply hone ke acknowledgement ka wait karna padta hai.
  Isliye Primary DB ko Application ko write success acknowledgement bhejne mein comparatively zyada samay lag sakta hai.
  Consequently, Application ko bhi Client/User ko write success response bhejne mein zyada samay lag sakta hai.
  Is wajah se overall write latency badh sakti hai, jisse user experience thoda degrade ho sakta hai.


2: PRIMARY DB KI REPLICA(S) PAR TIGHT COUPLING
------------------------------------------------

Primary DB ko Application ko write success acknowledgement dene se pehle,
configured replication policy ke according Replica(s) se required
acknowledgements milne ka wait karna padta hai.

Isliye Primary DB ki write acknowledgement Replica(s) ki
availability aur performance par depend kar sakti hai.

Agar required acknowledgements dene wali Replica(s) slow ho,
to unke acknowledgements late aa sakte hain, jiski wajah se
Primary DB ki write acknowledgement bhi delay ho sakti hai.

Agar Replica(s) temporarily unavailable ho jaayein, jiski wajah se
required acknowledgements receive na ho paayein, to Primary DB
configured timeout tak wait kar sakta hai.

Uske baad write request ke saath exactly kya behaviour hoga,
ye database ki replication configuration aur policy
par depend karta hai.


1. PRIMARY DB PAR WRITE COMMITTED REH SAKTI HAI, LEKIN CLIENT KO FAILURE / TIMEOUT RESPONSE MIL SAKTA HAI
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Primary DB ne apna local write commit kar diya hota hai, lekin 
   required Replica acknowledgements configured timeout tak receive nahi hue.
   Aisi situation mein Client ko failure ya timeout response mil sakta hai.

   Yahan Client ko lag sakta hai ki write fail ho gayi,
   lekin Primary DB par data actually persist ho chuka ho sakta hai.

   Client agar bina verify kiye blindly same write request retry kare,
   to duplicate write ka risk ho sakta hai.
   Isi wajah se practical distributed systems mein
   idempotency important hoti hai.

2. PRIMARY DB PAR WRITE ROLLBACK HO SAKTI HAI
-----------------------------------------------
    Agar required replication condition satisfy nahi hoti, to write ko successfully committed
    nahi maana jaata aur Primary DB par write rollback ki ja sakti hai.
    Is case mein Primary DB aur Client dono ke perspective se write unsuccessful hoti hai.




2. ASYNCHRONOUS REPLICATION 
-----------------------------

Is approach mein Primary DB par write successful hone ke baad,
Primary DB Replica(s) par change successfully replicate/apply hone
ka wait nahi karta aur Application ko write success ka acknowledgement
de deta hai.

Application uske baad Client ko success response de sakti hai.

Primary DB ke changes uske baad asynchronously Replica(s) tak
propagate/apply hote rehte hain.



## BENEFIT
------------

#1. LOWER WRITE LATENCY
------------------------
   Primary DB ko Replica(s) ke acknowledgement ka
   wait nahi karna padta.
   Isliye Primary DB Application ko write ka acknowledgement
   comparatively jaldi bhej sakta hai.


#2: PRIMARY DB KI REPLICA(S) PAR LOOSE COUPLING
------------------------------------------------
   Unlike Synchronous Replication, Asynchronous Replication mein
   Primary DB ko Replica(s) ke acknowledgement ka wait nahi karna padta.

   Isliye agar koi Read Replica temporarily slow ya unavailable hai,
   to Primary DB ko us Replica ke acknowledgement ka wait
   nahi karna padta.



## LIMITATION :
---------------

### 1. REPLICATION LAG
------------------------

Primary DB par write successful hone ke baad,
Replica par same change immediately available nahi hota hai.
Agar isi duration mein application Replica se read kare,
to user ko stale/outdated data mil sakta hai.


### 2. PRIMARY DB KE IMMEDIATE FAILURE KE CASE MEIN RECENT WRITE LOST HO SAKTI HAI
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Maan lo:

   Primary DB par write successfully complete ho gayi.

Lekin wo change abhi Replica(s) par apply nahi hua hai.

Primary DB ne Application ko write success ka acknowledgement
de diya.

Uske just baad Primary DB fail ho gaya.

Agar isi Replica ko new Primary bana diya jaata hai,
to recently acknowledged write us Replica par available
nahi ho hogi. Is situation mein system ke perspective se 
recently acknowledged write lost ho sakti hai.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: READ REPLICAS MEIN KAUNSA COMMON HOTA HAI — ASYNCHRONOUS YA SYNCHRONOUS?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Asynchronous Replication commonly use ki jaati hai kyunki Primary DB par write successful hone ke
baad Primary DB ko Replica(s) ke acknowledgement ka wait nahi karna padta.

Isliye Primary DB Application ko write success acknowledgement comparatively jaldi bhej sakta hai,
jisse Client ko write request ka success response jaldi milta hai aur client-facing write latency comparatively kam rehti hai.

Note: Write execution necessarily fast nahi hua; bas write request ka end-to-end acknowledgement fast hua.

Iska trade-off ye hai ki replication complete hone tak Replica Primary DB se peeche reh sakti hai, 
jisse meanwhile Replica se aane wali read requests mein stale (outdated) data mil sakta hai.


---------------------------------------
## QUESTION: REPLICATION LAG KYA HAI ?
---------------------------------------

Replication instant nahi hoti.

Primary DB par change hone aur Replica par
us change ke apply hone ke beech jo delay hota hai,
use Replication Lag kehte hain.

## EXAMPLE:
------------

User profile update:

    Old name: Anand
    New name: Anurag

Assume karo ki successful update ke baad
application ne corresponding Redis aur
application instances ke Local Cache entries
invalidate kar di hain.

Step 1:
    Primary DB par name successfully update ho gaya.

    Primary DB:
        name = Anurag

Step 2:
    Redis aur Local Cache me old value available nahi hai,
    isliye immediately updated name ke liye next read request
    Read Replica se serve ki jaati hai.

Step 3:
    Replica par abhi tak latest change apply nahi hua tha.

    Read Replica:
        name = Anand

Result:

    User ki request Read Replica se serve hoti hai
    aur user ko old name "Anand" milta hai.

    Yaani database ke Primary DB me data already updated hai,
    lekin replication lag ki wajah se user ki request par
    usse stale (outdated) data mil raha hai.

Is situation ko Stale Read kehte hain.


--------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: REPLICATION LAG KAISE HANDLE KARE ?
--------------------------------------------------

Common strategies:

#1. READ-YOUR-WRITE FROM PRIMARY DB
------------------------------------

    Agar user ne abhi data update kiya hai,
    to immediate next read primary DB se karao.

    Example:

        Update profile -> Primary DB
        Immediately fetch profile -> Primary DB
        Later reads -> Replica(s)

2. CRITICAL READS FROM PRIMARY DB
----------------------------------

    Important data primary database se read karo always.

    Example:

        Bank balance
        Payment status
        Order confirmation

    Jahan slight staleness acceptable ho,
    wahan reads Replica(s) se serve ki ja sakti hain.

    Example:

        Product listing
        Blog posts
        Catalog data
        Public feed


----------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: KYA REPLICATION EXPENSIVE HAI ?
----------------------------------------------

Haan, replication free nahi hoti.

Costs:

- Primary DB ko changes log karne padte hain
- Changes replicas tak network ke through jaate hain
- Replicas ko changes apply karne padte hain
- Additional storage required hota hai
- Replication lag ho sakta hai

Benefits:

- Primary DB se read load shift ho jaata hai
- System zyada read traffic handle kar sakta hai

So, replication ka cost hota hai, lekin read-heavy systems mein
ye cost often worth it hota hai.


--------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: PRIMARY DB FAIL HO JAAYE TO KYA HOTA HAI?
--------------------------------------------------------

Agar Primary DB fail ho jaaye, to Application naye writes perform nahi kar paayegi.
High Availability setup mein, system ek healthy Read Replica ko
new Primary ke role mein promote kar sakta hai.
Is process ko Failover kehte hain.

Uske baad:

Writes → New Primary

Aur remaining Replica(s) new Primary se replication continue karti hain.

Isliye Replication sirf Read Scaling ke liye nahi,
balki High Availability provide karne mein bhi help karti hai.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SYSTEM KO KAISE PATA CHALEGA KI PRIMARY DB FAIL HO GAYA HAI?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

System Primary DB ki availability ko periodically Health Checks ke through
monitor karta hai.

Yahan "System" se matlab un components se hai jo database infrastructure
ko monitor aur manage kar rahe hote hain.

System periodically Primary DB ko check karta hai:

    "Kya Primary DB reachable hai aur requests handle kar raha hai?"

Agar Primary DB multiple consecutive Health Checks ka response nahi deta,
to system usse unavailable ya suspected failed maan sakta hai.

Lekin yahan ek important problem hai.

Agar System ko Primary DB se response nahi mil raha hai,
to iska ye matlab zaroori nahi hai ki Primary DB actually fail ho gaya hai.

Primary DB actually running aur healthy ho sakta hai,
lekin temporary network ya connectivity issue ki wajah se
System ko Primary DB ka response nahi mil paa raha ho.

Is situation mein agar System immediately Primary ko failed maan kar
kisi Replica ko promote kar de, aur original Primary actually healthy
aur running ho, to ek hi time par multiple Primary databases active
ho sakte hain.

Is situation ko Split Brain kehte hain.

Isliye system ek single failed Health Check ke basis par
immediately failover nahi karta.

Multiple failed Health Checks aur configured timeouts ke basis par
decide kiya jaata hai ki Primary actually unavailable hai
ya temporary network issue ki wajah se unreachable hai.


------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SPLIT BRAIN EXACTLY KYA HOTA HAI?
------------------------------------------------

Split Brain ka simple matlab broadly yehi hai ki ek Database Cluster mein
ek hi time par 2 Primary nodes active ho jaate hain.

Ye situation tab ho sakti hai jab Primary DB aur Monitoring/Failover
System ke beech network connectivity issue aa jaaye.
Is wajah se Monitoring/Failover System ko Primary DB ka response nahi milta 
aur woh assume kar leta hai ki Primary DB fail ho gaya hai.
Lekin actually Primary DB healthy ho sakta hai aur abhi bhi writes 
handle kar raha hota hai.

Agar Monitoring/Failover System is situation mein kisi Read Replica 
ko New Primary promote kar de, to problem create ho sakti hai.

Ab:

- Old Primary bhi active hai aur writes accept kar raha hai.
- New Primary bhi active hai aur writes accept kar raha hai.

Yaani ek hi Database Cluster mein ek hi time par 2 nodes Primary ki tarah writes accept karne lagte hain.

Isi situation ko Split Brain kehte hain.


------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SPLIT BRAIN DANGEROUS KYUN HAI?
------------------------------------------------

Split Brain mein ek hi database cluster mein multiple nodes Primary ki tarah behave karke writes accept karne lagte hain, 
jisse data inconsistency aur conflicting writes ka risk hota hai.


## EXAMPLE :
--------------

Initially cluster mein ek Primary DB aur 3 Read Replicas hain.

                 Original Primary
                 Name = Rahul
                      |
                   Replication
                      |
        +-------------+-------------+
        |             |             |
        v             v             v
   Replica 1      Replica 2      Replica 3
   Name=Rahul    Name=Rahul      Name=Rahul


Ab maan lo temporary network problem ki wajah se Failover System
Original Primary DB ko unreachable maan leta hai.

Lekin Original Primary DB actually healthy hai aur
writes accept kar raha hai.

Failover System Replica 1 ko promote karke New Primary DB bana deta hai.

Ab ek hi time par:

    Original Primary DB           New Primary DB   
     (Still Active)                (Replica 1)

         WRITE                       WRITE

Dono independently writes accept kar sakte hain.

## WRITE 1 — ORIGINAL PRIMARY PAR
-----------------------------------

Ek request Original Primary DB par jaati hai:

UPDATE User
SET name = 'Anurag'
WHERE user_id = 101;

Original Primary DB ki state:

user_id = 101
name = Anurag


## WRITE 2 — NEW PRIMARY PAR
--------------------------------

Ab ek dusri request New Primary DB
(Previously Replica 1) par jaati hai:

UPDATE User
SET name = 'Anand'
WHERE user_id = 101;

New Primary DB ki state:

user_id = 101
name = Anand


## PROBLEM
------------

Ab dono Primary DB's ki state different ho gayi:

Original Primary DB:
Name = Anurag

New Primary DB:
Name = Anand


Do independent Primary DB's same logical data ke liye
apni-apni data history maintain kar rahe hain.



## IDEALLY DATA UPDATE HISTORY KYA HONI CHAHIYE THI?
-------------------------------------------------------

Agar dono writes ek hi Primary node par sequentially execute hui hoti,
to expected data history hoti:

Rahul
↓
Anurag
↓
Anand

Lekin Split Brain ki wajah se dono writes
different Primary DB's par execute ho gayi.

Original Primary DB ki history:

Rahul
↓
Anurag

New Primary DB ki history:

Rahul
↓
Anand


## NETWORK PROBLEM RESOLVE HO JAATI HAI
-------------------------------------------

Ab network connectivity restore ho jaati hai.

Lekin ab dono Primary DB's ke paas same data ki different state hai:

Original Primary DB:
Name = Anurag

New Primary DB:
Name = Anand

System ko ab decide karna hoga ki kaunsa node
authoritative Primary DB maana jaaye.


Agar system Original Primary DB ko authoritative maan leta hai,
to New Primary DB par accept hui:

Rahul → Anand
wali write discard ho sakti hai.


Aur agar system New Primary DB ko authoritative maan leta hai,
to Original Primary DB par accept hui:

Rahul → Anurag

wali write discard ho sakti hai.


Yaani network restore hone ke baad dono Primary DB's ko simply
synchronize karna straightforward nahi hai, kyunki system ko
decide karna pad sakta hai ki conflicting data history mein
kaunsi state authoritative hai.


----------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SPLIT BRAIN KO PREVENT KAISE KAREIN?
----------------------------------------------------

Split Brain prevent karne ka main goal ye ensure karna hota hai ki
database cluster mein ek hi time par sirf ek node Primary ki tarah
behave kare aur writes accept kare.

Agar Primary DB unavailable maana jaata hai aur kisi Replica ko
New Primary ke role mein promote kiya ja raha hai, to system ko
ensure karna hota hai ki Original Primary ab writes accept na kar sake.

Iske liye Failover mechanisms different techniques use karte hain.

1: FENCING
--------------
Failover ke time system Original Primary ko
network se isolate karke writes accept karne se stop karne ki koshish karta hai.


2. QUORUM / CONSENSUS BASED DECISION
--------------------------------------
Failover ka decision kisi ek single monitoring node ke observation ke basis par nahi liya jaata. 
Multiple monitoring/coordination nodes ki agreement ke baad hi Replica ko New Primary promote kiya jaata hai.


---------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: MANUAL VS AUTOMATIC FAILOVER ?
---------------------------------------------

Failover do tarah se ho sakta hai:

1. AUTOMATIC FAILOVER
-----------------------

Monitoring / Failover System Primary DB failure detect karke
automatically kisi healthy Replica ko New Primary ke role mein
promote kar deta hai.

2. MANUAL FAILOVER
---------------------

Human / DBA situation verify karke manually kisi healthy Replica ko
New Primary ke role mein promote karta hai.

Ye approach tab use ho sakti hai jab automatic failover configured
na ho, ya system failover decision ke liye human intervention
require karta ho.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: FAILOVER KE BAAD APPLICATION KO KAISE PATA CHALEGA KI NAYA PRIMARY DB KAUN HAI?
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Application ideally kisi specific Database node ka address hardcode nahi karti.

Write aur Read requests ke liye separate Database Endpoints ho sakte hain:

Application → Write Endpoint → Current Primary DB

Application → Read Endpoint → Available Read Replica(s)

Failover ke baad Primary DB change hone par,
Write Endpoint automatically New Primary DB ki taraf route ho jaata hai.

Read Endpoint ke peeche available healthy Read Replica(s) ke beech
read traffic distribute karne ke liye Load Balancing / Routing mechanism
use ho sakta hai.

Isliye Application ko manually specific Primary ya Read Replica
nodes ki information manage nahi karni padti.






=======================================================================
#4. WRITE-INTENSIVE APPLICATION KO KAISE SCALE KAREIN?   - SHARDING
=======================================================================

Read Replicas se reads multiple nodes ke across distribute ho gaye.
Lekin writes abhi bhi Primary DB par hi ja rahi hain.

Agar write traffic significantly increase ho jaaye, to saari writes ek hi
Primary DB ko handle karni padengi, aur woh eventually write bottleneck ban sakta hai.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: KYA HUM WRITES KO BHI MULTIPLE DATABASE NODES PAR DISTRIBUTE KAR SAKTE HAIN?
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose hum multiple Write Databases bana dein aur randomly
different write requests ko different database nodes par bhejne lagen:

                Application
                     |
          +----------+----------+
          |          |          |
          v          v          v
        WRITE DB-1  WRITE DB-2  WRITE DB-3


Problem ye hai ki agar same logical data ke writes different databases
par independently jaane lagen, to data consistency maintain karna
difficult ho sakta hai.


## EXAMPLE :
------------

Initial User state:

user_id = 101
name = Anurag

WRITE 1 → WRITE DB-1

UPDATE User
SET name = 'Anand'
WHERE user_id = 101;

WRITE 2 → WRITE DB-2

UPDATE User
SET name = 'Anuj'
WHERE user_id = 101;

Ab dono databases ne same user ke liye apni independent history
maintain kar li:

WRITE DB-1:

    Anurag → Anand


WRITE DB-2:

    Anurag → Anuj


Ideally, agar dono writes ek hi database par sequentially
execute hui hoti, to data history kuch aisi honi chahiye thi:

Anurag → Anand → Anuj

Lekin yahan same user ka data different database nodes par
independently update hua hai.

Ab Application ke liye ye determine karna difficult ho sakta hai ki:

"user_id = 101 ke liye correct/latest data kis database mein hai?"

Isliye writes ko multiple database nodes ke across distribute karne ke liye
humein ek deterministic routing rule chahiye.

Yaani system ko reliably pata hona chahiye:

"Application ko pata hona chahiye ki particular data kis Database node par store karna hai,
taaki future mein jab usi data ke liye read/write request aaye, to us request ko usi Database node par route kiya ja sake."

Isi concept ko Sharding solve karta hai.



--------------------------------
## QUESTION: SHARDING KYA HAI?
--------------------------------

Sharding ek database horizontal scaling technique hai jisme
large dataset ko multiple independent database nodes
(Shards) ke across divide kiya jaata hai.

Har Shard total dataset ka sirf ek subset store aur manage karta hai.

Application ke andar ya Application ke saamne/saath koi routing mechanism,
defined routing rule ke basis par decide karta hai ki particular data kis Shard mein store hoga.


## EXMAPLE:
-------------


                Application
                     |
                     v
              Shard Routing Logic
                     |
         +-----------+-----------+
         |           |           |
         v           v           v
     Shard 1      Shard 2      Shard 3


Suppose total users:

User 1 → User 1,000,000


Sharding ke baad:

Shard 1 → Users 1 - 333,333
Shard 2 → Users 333,334 - 666,666
Shard 3 → Users 666,667 - 1,000,000

Yaani har Shard total dataset ka sirf ek portion handle/store karta hai, 
jisse data aur write load multiple database nodes ke across distribute ho sakta hai.

Isse overall system ki total write capacity increase ho sakti hai.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: APPLICATION KO KAISE PATA CHALTA HAI KI REQUEST KO KAUNSE SHARD PAR ROUTE KARNA HAI? — SHARD KEY
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shard Key woh field/attribute hota hai jiske basis par system decide karta hai ki particular data 
kis Shard mein store hoga aur kis Shard se retrieve kiya jayega.

- Write/Update: Request correct Shard par route hoti hai.
- Read: System correct Shard identify karke data retrieve karta hai.


Shard Key request ke time automatically discover nahi hoti.

Application design ke time pehle se define kiya jaata hai ki
particular Entity/Table ke liye kaunsi field Shard Key hogi.


## REQUEST FLOW
-----------------

Client Request
      |
      v
  Controller
      |
      v
Service Layer
      |
      | User Entity ke liye
      | Shard Key = user_id
      v
Extract user_id
      |
      v
Apply Shard Routing Rule
      |
      v
Correct Shard



## EXAMPLE
-------------

Maan lo:

User Table → Shard Key = user_id

User:

user_id = 101
name = Anurag
email = anurag@example.com


## WRITE
----------

New User create karte time, Application user_id ko Shard Key ke roop mein use karke 
Shard Routing Rule apply karegi aur User ko corresponding Shard mein store karegi.


## FUTURE UPDATE
-------------------

Future mein same User ke liye Update request aane par, Application dobara 
user_id ko Shard Key ke roop mein use karke same Shard Routing Rule apply karegi.

Isliye particular user_id ke related writes consistently same Shard par route hote hain.



----------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SHARD KEY CHOOSE KARTE TIME KYA DEKHNA CHAHIYE ? 
----------------------------------------------------------------

1. DATA ACCESS PATTERN
------------------------

Sabse pehle ye dekhna chahiye ki kisi particular Entity ka data
application ki most requests mein usually kis field ke basis par
access kiya jaata hai.

Maan lo User entity ke liye most requests:

GET /users/{user_id}
UPDATE /users/{user_id}
DELETE /users/{user_id}

In requests mein user_id directly available hai.

Isliye hum:

User Table → Shard Key = user_id

choose kar sakte hain.

Ab Application user_id ke basis par Shard Routing Rule apply karke
correct Shard identify kar sakti hai.


## LEKIN AGAR REQUEST MEIN SHARD KEY AVAILABLE NA HO TO?
-----------------------------------------------------------

Maan lo occasionally ek request aati hai:

GET /users?email=anurag@example.com

Is request mein Application ke paas email hai,
lekin directly user_id available nahi hai.

Agar user_id Shard Key hai, to Application directly
correct Shard identify nahi kar sakta.

Aise case mein commonly kuch approaches use ki ja sakti hain.

1. SCATTER-GATHER QUERY : 
--------------------------- 

   Application request ko multiple, ya zarurat padne par
   all Shards par bhej sakti hai.

   Har Shard par query execute ho sakti hai:

   Find User WHERE email = anurag@example.com

   Jis Shard mein required User milta hai,
   wahan se result return kiya ja sakta hai.

   Jab correct Shard directly identify nahi kiya ja sakta aur query ko multiple
   Shards par execute karna padta hai, to woh Cross-Shard Query ka ek case hota hai.

   ## PROBLEM 
   ------------
   Ek logical request multiple Shards ko hit kar sakti hai.
   Isliye frequent queries ke liye Scatter-Gather expensive ho sakta hai 
   aur generally avoid karna preferable hota hai.


2. LOOKUP / DIRECTORY SERVICE
-------------------------------

   Ek separate lookup/directory mapping maintain ki ja sakti hai:

   email                       user_id      
   --------------------------------------
   anurag@example.com          101        

   Is approach mein Application pehle email ke basis par corresponding user_id
   retrieve karti hai.

   Uske baad user_id ko Shard Key ke roop mein use karke
   Shard Routing Rule apply kiya jaata hai aur correct Shard
   identify kiya jaata hai.



2. DATA DISTRIBUTION
----------------------
  
  Shard Key aisi honi chahiye jisse data aur request traffic
  multiple Shards ke across reasonably distribute ho sake.

  ## EXAMPLE:
  ------------

  Shard Key = country

  India Users  → Very High
  US Users     → High
  Others       → Low

  Agar majority users India se hain, to India-related data ek hi Shard par bahut zyada accumulate ho sakta hai.
  to us Shard par disproportionatel zyada data aur requests aa sakti hain.

  ## RESULT:
  -----------

    Uneven Data / Traffic Distribution
            ↓
      One Shard receives
    significantly more load
            ↓
         Hot Shard
            ↓
        Bottleneck


## IMPORTANT
--------------

Koi universally perfect Shard Key nahi hoti.

Shard Key choose karte time application ke Data Access Patterns aur
Data/Traffic Distribution dono consider karne chahiye.

Agar Shard Key suitable nahi hui, to future mein:

- Uneven Load
- Hot Shards
- Scaling Bottlenecks

jaise problems aa sakti hain.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: SHARD KEY KE BASIS PAR SYSTEM KAISE DECIDE KARTA HAI KI DATA KAUNSE SHARD PAR JAAYEGA? — SHARD ROUTING STRATEGIES
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shard Key identify karne ke baad, system ko ek routing strategy ki zarurat hoti hai
jiske basis par Shard Key ki value ko kisi specific Shard se map kiya ja sake.

Common Shard Routing Strategies:

1. Range-Based Sharding
2. Hash-Based Sharding
3. Consistent Hashing
4. Directory / Lookup-Based Sharding



1. RANGE-BASED SHARDING
-------------------------

Range-Based Sharding mein Shard Key ki values ko different
predefined ranges mein divide kiya jaata hai.

Har range ko ek particular Shard assign kiya jaata hai.


Maan lo:

Shard Key = user_id

Aur user_id Shard Key ke liye ranges kuch aise define ki gayi hain:


Shard 1:

    user_id → 1 to 333,333


Shard 2:

    user_id → 333,334 to 666,666


Shard 3:

    user_id → 666,667 to 1,000,000


Request ki Shard Key value jis predefined range mein aati hai,
request us corresponding Shard par route ki jaati hai.



## RANGE-BASED SHARDING KA MAJOR PROBLEM — HOT SHARD
--------------------------------------------------------

Range-Based Sharding mein Shard Key ki values ko predefined ranges mein
divide kiya jaata hai aur har range ek particular Shard ko assign hoti hai.

Agar kisi particular range ka data disproportionately zyada ho jaaye ya us range ke data 
ke liye requests disproportionately zyada aane lagen, to us range ko handle karne wale Shard
par baaki Shards ke comparison mein significantly zyada load aa sakta hai.

Aise disproportionately overloaded Shard ko Hot Shard kehte hain.

## EXAMPLE 1:
---------------

Shard 1 → user_id 1 to 333,333
Shard 2 → user_id 333,334 to 666,666
Shard 3 → user_id 666,667 to 1,000,000

Ab maan lo user_id sequentially generate ho raha hai

Isliye naye User creation requests aur unse se related
write (Update) traffic disproportionately Shard 1 par aa sakta hai,
jabki baaki Shards comparatively kam utilized ya idle ho sakte hain.

Aisi situation mein ek Shard baaki Shards ke comparison mein
significantly zyada load handle karne lagta hai and hot shard ban jata hai.


## EXAMPLE 2:
--------------

Shard Key = country

Agar:

India   → Very High Traffic
US      → Medium Traffic
Others  → Low Traffic


Agar India-related data ke liye Write Requests bahut high hain,
to India ki Shard Key value ke corresponding Shard par
baaki Shards ke comparison mein disproportionately zyada
Write Traffic aa sakta hai.

Jabki baaki Shards comparatively kam Write Traffic handle kar
rahe ho sakte hain.

Is wajah se India-related data ko handle karne wala Shard
disproportionately zyada load handle karne lag sakta hai
and hot shard ban sakta hai.



## QUESTION: HOT SHARD KO HANDLE KAISE KIYA JA SAKTA HAI? - REBALANCING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Agar kisi particular Shard par disproportionately zyada write
traffic aa raha hai, to us Shard ki existing range ko split kiya
ja sakta hai.

Us range ke data ka ek portion naye ya comparatively less loaded
Shard mein move kiya ja sakta hai.

Is process ko Rebalancing kehte hain.


## EXAMPLE:
-------------

Predefined user_id ranges:

Shard 1 → user_id 1 to 100
Shard 2 → user_id 101 to 200
Shard 3 → user_id 201 to 300


Maan lo kisi reason se Shard 3 par disproportionately zyada
traffic aa raha hai:

Shard 1 → 20,000 requests/sec
Shard 2 → 25,000 requests/sec
Shard 3 → 90,000 requests/sec


Is situation mein Shard 3 ki existing range ko split kiya
ja sakta hai aur uske data ka ek portion naye Shard mein
move kiya ja sakta hai.


Before:

Shard 3 → user_id 201 to 300


After:

Shard 3 → user_id 201 to 250

Shard 4 → user_id 251 to 300


Is process mein user_id 251 to 300 ke existing data ko
Shard 3 se Shard 4 mein migrate/redistribute karna padega.

Saath hi, Shard Routing configuration ko bhi update karna padega,
taaki:

user_id 201 to 250 → Shard 3

user_id 251 to 300 → Shard 4


Isi process ko Rebalancing kehte hain.

Lekin Rebalancing process potentially expensive aur operationally
complex ho sakti hai, kyunki existing data migrate karna aur
routing configuration update karni pad sakti hai.




2. HASH-BASED SHARDING
-------------------------

Hash-Based Sharding mein Shard Key ki value par ek Hash Function apply kiya jaata hai.

Hash Function ka output use karke system decide karta hai ki data kis Shard par store ya retrieve kiya jayega.


## EXAMPLE:
------------

Maan lo:

Shard Key = user_id

Total Shards = 3


Routing rule:

hash(user_id) % Number of Shards


## BENEFIT
------------

Hash-Based Sharding Shard Key values ko multiple Shards ke across
relatively evenly distribute karne mein help karta hai.

Isliye sequential Shard Key values bhi different Shards par
distribute ho sakti hain, jisse Hot Shard ka risk reduce ho sakta hai.

Example:

user_id = 101 → Hash → Shard 2
user_id = 102 → Hash → Shard 0
user_id = 103 → Hash → Shard 1
user_id = 104 → Hash → Shard 2


## IMPORTANT:
--------------

Hash-Based Sharding Hot Shard problem ko completely eliminate nahi karti,
lekin generally Range-Based Sharding ke comparison mein
data aur load ko more evenly distribute karne mein help karti hai.


## LIMITATION
---------------

Hash-Based Sharding ki major limitation tab aati hai jab
cluster mein Shards ki total number change hoti hai.

Maan lo :

Routing Rule:

hash(shard_key) % number of shards

Initially:

Total Shards = 3

Ab system ko scale karne ke liye ek naya Shard add kiya:

Total Shards = 4

Ab same `user_id` ke liye routing result change ho sakta hai.

Example:

user_id = 101

Initially:

101 % 3 = 2

So:

user_id = 101 → Shard 2


Isliye `user_id = 101` ka data initially Shard 2 mein stored hai.

Ab ek naya Shard add karne ke baad routing rule change ho jaata hai:

101 % 4 = 1

Ab:

user_id = 101 → Shard 1

Yahan problem ye hai ki user_id = 101 ka existing data abhi bhi
Shard 2 mein stored hai.

Lekin naya Routing Rule apply karne ke baad future requests ke liye:

user_id = 101 → Shard 1

route calculate hoga.

Isliye future mein user_id = 101 ke liye aane wali Write (Update / Delete) requests,
Shard 1 par route ho sakti hain, jabki existing data abhi bhi Shard 2 mein present hai.

Yaani Routing Rule aur actual data location ke beech mismatch create ho jaata hai.

Is problem ko resolve karne ke liye `user_id = 101` ka existing data
Shard 2 se Shard 1 mein migrate karna padega.

Ye data migration expensive aur operationally complex ho sakti hai.

Isliye Shards ki number change hone par potentially large amount of
existing data ko migrate/redistribute karna pad sakta hai.

Isi problem ko efficiently handle karne ke liye
Consistent Hashing use ki ja sakti hai.




3. CONSISTENT HASHING
------------------------

Consistent Hashing bhi Hash-Based Sharding ki tarah Shard Key ke basis par data 
ko multiple Shards ke across distribute karne mein help karta hai.
Lekin iska main advantage ye hai ki jab Database Cluster mein Shards add ya remove hote hain,
to Hash-Based Sharding ke comparison mein relatively kam existing data ko migrate karna padta hai.


## QUESTION: CONSISTENT HASHING KAISE WORK KARTI HAI ?
-------------------------------------------------------

Consistent Hashing mein:

- Shard Nodes ko bhi hash kiya jaata hai.
- Shard Key value ko bhi hash kiya jaata hai.
- Dono ko ek fixed Hash Ring par place kiya jaata hai.
- Uske baad Shard Key ki position se clockwise direction mein jo
  pehla Shard Node milta hai, data us Shard ko assign hota hai.


## QUESTION: HASH SPACE AUR HASH RING KYA HAI ?
--------------------------------------------------

Ek Hash Function kisi input ko ek fixed numeric range ke andar ek value mein
convert karta hai.

hash(input) → 0 to 359 ke beech koi value

Is fixed numeric range ko Hash Space kehte hain.

Real systems mein ye range bahut large hoti hai.

Conceptually, is Hash Space ko circular form mein represent kiya ja sakta hai.

Kyuki Hash Space ko circular form mein represent kiya jaata hai,
ise Hash Ring kehte hain.


## EXMAPLE
------------

Maan lo based on our Hash function hamara Hash Space hai:

0 ------------------------------ 359

Ab hum isko circular form mein represent karte hain:


                    0 / 360
                       |
                       |
          300          |          60
             \         |         /
              \        |        /
